Late Delivery Risk Prediction

This project applies machine learning to predict whether an order will be delivered late based on order, shipping, regional, and product category features. The goal is to identify high-risk deliveries early and uncover supply chain factors contributing to delays.

Project Overview
Late deliveries impact customer satisfaction and operational efficiency. In this project, a Random Forest classification model is built to estimate late delivery risk and analyze patterns across different product categories, shipping modes, and regions.

Dataset
The dataset from Kaggle contains historical order and shipping information, including:
- Order type  
- Shipping mode  
- Order region  
- Scheduled shipment days  
- Product category  
- Late delivery risk (target variable)

Methodology
1. Performed exploratory data analysis (EDA) to identify high-risk categories and delivery patterns.  
2. Selected relevant features based on EDA insights.  
3. Applied one-hot encoding to categorical variables.  
4. Split the data into training and testing sets (80/20).  
5. Trained a Random Forest classifier with controlled depth to reduce overfitting.  
6. Evaluated the model using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and a confusion matrix.

Results
- Achieved approximately 69% accuracy on unseen test data.  
- High precision in predicting late deliveries, indicating reliable identification of high-risk orders.    

Key Insights
- Certain product categories consistently show higher late delivery risk.  
- Shipping mode and region play a significant role in delivery performance.  
- Delivery delays are largely driven by supply chain and logistics constraints rather than random variation.

Technologies Used
- Python  
- pandas, NumPy  
- scikit-learn  
- matplotlib  
